uential for those with health  problems like metabolic syndrome a group  of health issues like how high blood  sugar and obesity that in turn can  increase your chances of developing  certain chronic illnesses like diabetes  and cardiovascular disease a recent  study published in the european journal  of nutrition referred to estimates  showing nearly 35 percent of all  american adults and half of those 60 and  older suffered from metabolic syndrome  in 2011 and 2012 the study looked at a  small group of patients who received  some dietary counseling while following  the american heart association's 2006  dietary guidelines and examined if this  diet was effective in decreasing added  sugar tara what did this study show us  right so I think it really speaks to the  power of nutritional support and dietary  counseling and I tell a lot of my  patients they should work with the  nutritionist I think that's why programs  like Weight Watchers or Jenny Craig can  be successful because it gives people  that added support so in this study  there were 119 people who were given  information about  AAA's diet but also received individual  and group counseling sessions over the  course of a year at the end of that year  they were able to decrease their added  sugar by about 24 grams which sounds  great and it is great but the problem is  that about 48 percent were still over  the recommended amount which for men is  about 9 teaspoons a day or 36 grams of  added sugar and for women about 6  teaspoons a day or 24 grams of added  sugar  they sneak sugar into every finally time  for some practical advice this week the  importance of knowing your family's  medical history John and Tara how just  how far back do we need to go here yeah  I think this is so important people  don't give it a lot of the credit that  it deserves so many times I ask my  patients you know tell me about your  mother and father or your brother and  sister and they have no idea but  especially in cardiovascular disease it  plays a big role in determining your  risk for things like coronary artery  disease heart failure electrical  abnormalities and it really helps us  guide how we diagnose people what tests  we might order and how we treat them  preventively yeah and it's not you know  people think about heart disease and  cancer but it's other things it's Galit  it's kidney stones it's mental illness  there are all sorts of things that you  can get clues at and of course one of  the problems is that back then people  didn't they weren't that open about  their problems so often somebody will  come to me and they say well I don't  really know I think she had stomach  cancer  well stomach cancer could be colon  cancer which would mean they have to get  screened maybe earlier so try to get as  many details as you can ask questions  don't take no for an answer all right  doctors Jon LaPook and Tara Narula  thanks
 

